<DocsLayout
  title="Documentation"
  description="Learn how to create audio-reactive motion graphics with Astrofox."
>
  <p>
    Astrofox is a free, browser-based motion graphics program that turns music
    into visuals. Drop in an audio file, layer displays and effects, wire them
    up to audio reactors, and export a high-definition video.
  </p>

  <h2>New here?</h2>
  <p>Start with these three pages to get a feel for the app:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><a href="/docs/interface">Interface tour</a> &mdash; the main panels and what they do.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/quick-start">Quick start</a> &mdash; build your first visualization in a few minutes.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/reactors">Reactors</a> &mdash; how audio drives motion.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How it fits together</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>project</strong> contains one or more <strong>scenes</strong>.
    Each scene holds a stack of <strong>displays</strong> (the visual elements
    like text, shapes and spectrums) and <strong>effects</strong> (post-processing
    filters like blur, glitch and color). <strong>Reactors</strong> analyze the
    audio and output a value that can be bound to any numeric property on a
    display or effect &mdash; that's what makes things move to the beat.
  </p>

  <h2>Launch the app</h2>
  <p>
    Astrofox runs in the browser at
    <a href="https://app.astrofox.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener">app.astrofox.io</a>.
    There's nothing to install.
  </p>

